   "solo_text": "# Hash Map Collision Handling\n\n## What Is a Collision?\n\nA **collision** occurs when two different keys hash to the same index in the underlying array. Since the hash function maps an infinite key space to a finite array size, collisions are inevitable.\n\n## Common Collision Resolution Strategies\n\n| Strategy | Idea | Trade-off |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Separate Chaining** | Each bucket holds a linked list (or other container) of entries | Simple; extra memory for pointers |\n| **Open Addressing** | Probe for the next empty slot (linear, quadratic, double hashing) | Cache-friendly; clustering risk |\n| **Robin Hood Hashing** | Open addressing variant that rebalances probes | Fewer worst-case probes |\n\nBelow, we focus on **separate chaining**, the most common and intuitive approach.\n\n## How Separate Chaining Works\n\n1. Compute `index = hash(key) % array_size`.\n2. If",
